<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dominican Liberation Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1973">1973</a>–present</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dominican Liberation Party is a political party of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>, founded in 1973 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bosch">Juan Bosch</a> after he left the Dominican Revolutionary Party. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonel-fernandez">Leonel Fernández</a> (1996–2000 and 2004–2012) and Danilo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> (2012–2020) it held the presidency for most of two decades. It lost power in 2020, after a split in which Fernández formed a new party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dominican-liberation-party.jpg" alt="Dominican Liberation Party" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WikiMaker45 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_del_Partido_de_la_Liberacion_Dominicana.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party's founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bosch">Juan Bosch</a>, entered politics in the late 1920s and opposed Rafael Leonidas Trujillo, who took power in 1930. Arrested in 1934 on suspicion of conspiracy and released, he later left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> in self-imposed exile. After Trujillo's assassination he returned and in 1963 became the country's first democratically elected president, but the military overthrew him after a few months. A civil war and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> intervention followed, and Joaquin Balaguer, one of Trujillo's original supporters, won the presidency.</p><p>Bosch rejoined the Dominican Revolutionary Party but left it in the early 1970s, feeling that it worked only for small and wealthy interest groups and not for the people. The PLD he founded in 1973, with a yellow five-pointed star on a purple background as its emblem, stood considerably to the left of the PRD. Bosch ran as its presidential candidate several times between 1978 and 1994 without winning, and retired from politics in 1996 at the age of 87.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonel-fernandez">Leonel Fernández</a>, Bosch's running mate in 1994, succeeded him as leader and won the presidential election of 1996 in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a>, for more than 30 years Bosch's political enemy. Term-limited in 2000, he returned to office in 2004 with 57.1% of the vote and won again in 2008 with 54%. In the legislative elections of 2006 the party led the Progressive Bloc to 96 of 178 deputies and 22 of 32 senators, and in 2010 it won 31 of the 32 Senate seats.</p><p>Danilo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> won the presidency for the party in 2012 with 51.2% of the vote against Hipólito Mejía of the PRD, and again in 2016. The Odebrecht scandal, in which the Brazilian construction company paid more than 90 million dollars in bribes to the Dominican government between 2001 and 2014, spanned the presidencies of Mejía, Fernández and Medina; it caused wide distrust and gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-march">Green March</a> protest movement.</p><p>At the election of 5 July 2020, postponed from May by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covid-19-pandemic">coronavirus pandemic</a>, the PLD candidate Gonzalo Castillo won 37% against 54% for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-abinader">Luis Abinader</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modern-revolutionary-party">Modern Revolutionary Party</a>, ending sixteen years of PLD presidencies. Fernández, writing in an op-ed, put the defeat down to several factors, among them the Odebrecht scandal and protests against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>'s government.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dominican Liberation Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Leonel Fernández and Danilo Medina of the <b>Dominican Liberation Party</b> then governed for most of the next two decades.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a></li></ul></section>
